  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM CDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nA broad upper trough was noted over the northern half of the CONUS \non the 19Z water vapor imagery with a subtle wave moving over the \nmiddle MO river valley. A second shortwave was digging southeast over\nthe northern Rockies. Surface obs had an area of low pressure across\nnorthwest OK with an inverted trough axis extending through \nnortheast KS and into southeast IA. This boundary had been nearly \nstationary for much of the day. \n\nFor the next few days the forecast will be dependent on shortwave \nenergy passing over the surface boundary. And the latest model \ntrends have these waves being more subtle than earlier runs. As a \nresult the models and CAMs have tended to back off on coverage of \nshower activity through Thursday. Along and ahead of the boundary, \nmodels still show a modestly unstable airmass with bulk shear around \n30KT. So the chances for showers and storms will persist but with \nthe lower coverage, POPs have come down somewhat. The one \nopportunity for severe storms may be Thursday afternoon across parts \nof east central KS where the models show some heating along the \nfront as it washes out. But confidence in destabilization with \nexpected cloud cover is somewhat marginal. There is still a \nshortwave progged to move through the northwest flow Thursday night \nand now the NBM shows the higher POPs for Thursday night. \n\nHeight rises over the central Rockies and into the central Plains \nlate Friday and this weekend are expected to push the mean \nwesterlies back north with dry and hot conditions developing. The \nNBM still has highs for Sunday and Monday in the middle 90s while \nthe 00Z ensemble data suggests some potential for highs in the \ntriple digits. Even the 12Z operational runs show 925MB temps \nwarming to around +35C across the western counties. So there \ncontinues to be some room for highs Sunday and Monday to trend \nwarmer. Models show the upper ridge weakening by the middle of next \nweek. This may allow another boundary to move into the central plains \nby Wednesday with slightly cooler temps and chances for showers and \nstorms. Until then, chances for precip this weekend and early next \nweek look to be driven by mesoscale processes and generally around \n15 percent or less.",
